      "body": "## Motivation\n\n`TopKConfig.correction_bias_for_dtype` caches `e_score_correction_bias` cast to the activation dtype. The cache key includes the parameter's `_version`, which is the mechanism the existing comment relies on to drop a copy taken before weights are loaded.\n\nThat check does not fire for the writes RL actually uses. Weight loaders and the RL weight update path write through `param.data.copy_()`, and a write through `.data` does not bump `_version`. The cached BF16 bias built during warmup therefore stays in place for the rest of the process. With dummy-load startup, warmup runs on random weights, so every later routing decision uses a correction bias of about `±1e-3` instead of the real values (about 5 per expert on GLM-5.3-Flash). Weight sync replaces the parameter and the weight checker never sees the cache, because it is not a registered tensor.\n\nThis affected every RL run on the ROCm GLM-5.3-Flash branch, including MI355X, not only MI300X. It is not an AMD kernel bug. Any in-place weight update that writes `e_score_correction_bias` through `.data` leaves the cache stale, on CUDA as well as HIP.\n\n## Modifications\n\nWhen the cache key matches, copy the current bias into the existing cached tensor instead of returning it unchanged. The copy keeps the CUDA-graph address stable. A real `_version` change still allocates a new tensor, which is what `test_cache_refreshes_if_weight_is_reloaded` already checks.\n\n`test_data_write_refreshes_cache_in_place` covers the `.data.copy_()` case: values update, and the cached storage address does not move.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nGLM-5.3-Flash BF16 RL, 4 steps, same data, MI300X. `train_rollout_logprob_abs_diff` per step:\n\n| Run | Gap |\n|---|---|\n| Before this fix | 0.153, 0.150, 0.101, 0.157 |\n| After this fix | 0.087, 0.085, 0.061, 0.094 |\n\nThe fixed gap matches the previous rollout build (0.089, 0.065, 0.094). Comparing a disk-loaded server with a dummy-loaded server, the correction-bias cache was the only tensor that differed across 1,513 checked tensors: real bias summed to about 1525, the dummy cache to about 0.\n\nThe new unit test could not be executed on this machine (no local torch). It is a CPU test registered in `base-a-test-cpu`.\n\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\n\nThe refresh is one `copy_` of the bias vector (288 elements on GLM-5.3-Flash) when the cache key hits. Rollout step time on the fixed MI300X run was 114–157 s, in the same range as the previous build (120–138 s).\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format your code according to the [Format code with pre-commit](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#format-code-with-pre-commit).\n- [x] Add unit tests according to the [Run and add unit tests](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#run-and-add-unit-tests).\n- [ ] Update documentation according to [Write documentations](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#write-documentations).\n- [x] Provide accuracy and speed benchmark results according to [Test the accuracy](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#test-the-accuracy) and [Benchmark the speed](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#benchmark-the-speed).\n- [x] Follow the SGLang code style [guidance](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#code-style-guidance).\n\n\nMade with [Cursor](https://cursor.com)\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #38785869731](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38785869731)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #38785869002](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38785869002)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #38785869329](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38785869329)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
